<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuchen Guo
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution. 
<br class="ltx_break">Correspondence to: yuchenguo2027@u.northwestern.edu, wfsu@bnbu.edu.cn.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Northwestern University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Junli Gong<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Northeastern University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yao Lu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Washington
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xintong Xu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing Normal - Hong Kong Baptist University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yiuming Cheung
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Hong Kong Baptist University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weifeng Su
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing Normal - Hong Kong Baptist University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Infrared-Visible image fusion (IVIF) aims to integrate thermal information and detailed spatial structures into a single fused image to enhance perception. However, existing evaluation approaches tend to over-optimize both hand-crafted no-reference statistics and full-reference metrics that treat the source images as pseudo ground truths. Recent IVIF
reward-modelling efforts learn from human ratings but use scalar regression on aggregated scores, neither leveraging the reasoning of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) nor encoding per-image perceptual ambiguity in their supervision, but naively introducing MLLMs with discrete one-hot supervision likewise collapses fused images of similar quality into different rating levels. To address this, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">FuScore</span>, which utilizes an MLLM to mimic human visual perception by producing <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuous</span> quality score, rather than discrete level predictions, enabling fine-grained discrimination among fused images of similar quality. We exploit the agreement among four IVIF-specific sub-dimensions to construct a per-image soft label whose sharpness reflects how consensual the overall judgment is. We further introduce a tripartite objective combining per-image distributional supervision, within-source-pair Thurstone fidelity for method-level ordering, and cross-source-pair Thurstone fidelity for scene-level ordering across scenes. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FuScore achieves state-of-the-art correlation with human visual preferences.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Infrared-Visible image fusion (IVIF) is an important technique in image processing, which aims to combine both thermal and structural information from two source images into a single fused image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. By integrating complementary thermal and structural cues into a unified image representation, IVIF has been widely applied in a variety of real-world scenarios, including security surveillance, military target detection, scene understanding, and assisted driving. Existing IVIF models can be broadly categorized into two approaches: discriminative methods such as carefully designed CNN-based and Transformer-based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>; generative approaches, including autoencoder-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, GAN-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and diffusion-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the rapid advancement of deep learning, above IVIF models have achieved substantial progress in improving fusion quality. However, a fundamental limitation remains: these existing methods are still predominantly optimized for hand-crafted traditional no-reference statistics such as Entropy (EN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and Mutual Information (MI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, or toward full-reference objectives that treat source image pairs as pseudo ground truths, such as Structural Similarity Index (SSIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, due to the absence of authentic ground-truth fused images in IVIF. As a result, these objectives often fail to faithfully reflect human experts perceptual preference, and may even assign higher scores to a visually inferior fused image (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.06969v1/intro.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/128;" width="476" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Two limitations of current IVIF quality assessment.
<span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) Misranking within the same scene:</span> for a single
source pair, traditional no-reference metrics (VIF, EN, SD,
SF) rank the human-disfavoured fused image B above the
human-preferred A (MOS <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{A}{=}3.50&gt;\mu_{B}{=}2.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.50</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{A}{=}3.50&gt;\mu_{B}{=}2.75</annotation></semantics></math>).
<span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) One-hot MLLM-as-Judge lacks fidelity:</span> two fused
images with near-tie human MOS (<math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.75</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.50</annotation></semantics></math>) are
forced into distinct discrete levels (<math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>) by an
MLLM trained with one-hot supervision over five quality
levels.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A recent IVIF reward-modelling effort, EVAFusion
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, addresses the human-alignment gap by
training a ViT-based regressor with MSE on aggregated overall
scores. Yet it neither leverages the linguistic reasoning of
MLLMs nor encodes how <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">consensual</em> each image’s mean
score is across the underlying multi-criterion judgments. Another
naive remedy of introducing MLLMs with the discrete one-hot
supervision used in prior MLLM-based IQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> is unable to capture subtle perceptual differences and carries no signal about per-image annotation consensus either. This leaves a fundamental question largely unanswered: <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">how can MLLMs produce continuous IVIF quality scores under
supervision that encodes per-image annotation consensus?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We answer this question with <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">FuScore</span>, an
MLLM-based scorer that yields a continuous quality score for
each fused image rather than forcing it into a single discrete
level. Specifically, instead of training the MLLM to emit a
single quality token, we read out the decoding probabilities
over the predefined quality-level tokens and supervise this
distribution with a soft label, so that the final score,
computed as the expectation under the predicted distribution,
can take any real value within the rating range and capture
subtle quality differences that one-hot supervision collapses.
A key challenge, however, lies in how to construct the soft
label itself: naively smoothing a discrete overall score with a
fixed-width Gaussian ignores the fact that fused images vary
substantially in how consensual their perceptual quality is.
To address this, we exploit the structure of existing IVIF
annotations, where each fused image is labeled along four
sub-dimensions: thermal retention, texture preservation,
artifacts, and sharpness, and use the agreement among these
sub-dimensions as a per-image consensus signal. When the four
criteria agree, the soft label is sharply concentrated around
the overall score; when they disagree, the label is broadened
to reflect that the overall judgment is less consensual across
reasonable aggregation rules. We further observe that
subjective fusion quality exhibits both within-source-pair
variation, which captures how different fusion methods perform
on the same scene, and cross-source-pair variation, which
reflects scene-dependent difficulty. Since neither per-image
supervision nor within-scene comparisons alone can cover both
variation components, FuScore adopts a tripartite objective
that jointly enforces per-image distributional supervision,
within-source-pair Thurstone fidelity for method-level ordering,
and cross-source-pair Thurstone fidelity for scene-level
ordering, with the per-pair margin reusing the per-image label
widths derived from sub-dimension agreement. Our core
contributions are summarized as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose FuScore, the first MLLM-based IVIF quality
scorer trained with distribution-level supervision, generating continuous score of fused images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a sub-dimension-aware soft label construction
that modulates label sharpness by the agreement among four
IVIF-specific sub-dimensions, allowing the supervision target to
faithfully reflect how consensual the overall perceptual
judgment is on each fused image.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a tripartite training objective that jointly
enforces per-image distributional supervision, within-source-pair
Thurstone fidelity for method-level ordering, and
cross-source-pair Thurstone fidelity for scene-level ordering,
with the per-pair margin reusing the per-image label widths
derived from sub-dimension agreement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the EVAFusion benchmark and a 5-expert Semantic RT
pilot, FuScore achieves state-of-the-art correlation with human
visual preferences across all reported rank metrics, and
empirically tracks both data-side ranking difficulty and
human-side inter-rater disagreement.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Infrared-Visible Image Fusion.</span>
Infrared-visible image fusion (IVIF) is a crucial field of
low-level computer vision. Early CNN-based encoder-decoder
architectures such as IFCNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> rely on
hand-designed fusion rules, while Transformer-based models like
SwinFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> capture long-range
cross-modality dependencies via self-attention. Generative
approaches instead model the data distribution of fused images:
autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>
learn modality-specific representations,
GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> encourage perceptual
realism via adversarial training, and
diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> formulates fusion as
conditional generation. Despite diverse architectures, all
optimize either no-reference statistics or pseudo-reference
objectives derived from source pairs, both poorly correlated
with human perception, motivating a perceptually aligned
quality scorer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Quality Assessment.</span>
IQA methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> predict a per-image quality
score under no-reference or full-reference paradigms. MLLMs have
recently been adapted as perceptual scorers on natural images:
Q-Align <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> fine-tunes an MLLM to predict five
discrete quality levels via level-token probabilities, and
DeQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> replaces one-hot supervision with
soft-label distributions from inter-rater variance. In IVIF,
fusion quality is still commonly evaluated using handcrafted
metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, no-reference measures such as
EN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, MI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>,
and VIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, or full-reference
SSIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and PSNR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> treating
source pairs as pseudo references. Recent IVIF reward-modelling efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>
trains a ViT-based regressor with MSE on aggregated overall
scores; it is, however, neither MLLM-based nor
distribution-supervised, and its scalar regression target
provides no signal about per-image perceptual consensus.
MLLM-based perceptual scorers and per-image ambiguity
modelling have not yet been explored for IVIF.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FuScore</span>, an MLLM-based scorer that assigns each fused
image a continuous quality score reflecting human perceptual preference.
We first formulate the scoring problem and the MLLM output formulation
(Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Setup ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), then introduce our sub-dimension-aware soft
label construction (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and finally describe
the tripartite training objective that jointly supervises per-image
distributional matching, within-source-pair fidelity, and cross-source-pair
ranking (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input and supervision.</span>
We leverage EVAFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, a large-scale IVIF
quality benchmark comprising <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9{,}350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9,350</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9{,}350</annotation></semantics></math> fused images produced by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>
representative IVIF methods over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="850" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>850</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">850</annotation></semantics></math> source image pairs. For each
fused image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, EVAFusion provides aggregated human ratings along
four IVIF-specific sub-dimensions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{i}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{i}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (thermal
retention, texture preservation, artifacts, and sharpness), together
with an overall quality score <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, all in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,5]</annotation></semantics></math>. The model input is source-conditioned and full-reference:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}=(\mathrm{IR}_{i},\ \mathrm{VIS}_{i},\ \mathrm{Fused}_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>IR</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>VIS</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Fused</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}=(\mathrm{IR}_{i},\ \mathrm{VIS}_{i},\ \mathrm{Fused}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so that the scorer evaluates the fused image jointly with its two source
modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From discrete tokens to a continuous score.</span>
We instantiate FuScore on a pretrained MLLM
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the input <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a quality-query prompt,
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> produces logits <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> over five predefined quality-level
tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\{1,2,3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\{1,2,3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> at the decoding step. Applying softmax
over these five tokens yields a categorical distribution</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}(l\,|\,\theta)=\frac{\exp(z_{i}^{(l)})}{\sum_{l^{\prime}\in\mathcal{L}}\exp(z_{i}^{(l^{\prime})})},\qquad l\in\mathcal{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}(l\,|\,\theta)=\frac{\exp(z_{i}^{(l)})}{\sum_{l^{\prime}\in\mathcal{L}}\exp(z_{i}^{(l^{\prime})})},\qquad l\in\mathcal{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The final continuous score is then read out as the expectation of the
quality level under this distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}=\mathbb{E}_{q_{i}}[L]=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}l\cdot q_{i}(l)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}=\mathbb{E}_{q_{i}}[L]=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}l\cdot q_{i}(l).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This expectation lies in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,5]</annotation></semantics></math> and varies continuously with the predicted
distribution, so that perceptually similar fused images can be assigned
distinct fine-grained scores rather than being collapsed into the same
discrete level. Importantly, the model emits a single distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
rather than separately predicting a variance term: all uncertainty
modeling thus enters via the supervision target’s width (described next),
while predictive uncertainty at inference is read off as the standard
deviation of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and used in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To supervise the predicted distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a per-image
soft label <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over the five quality levels. The center of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
determined by the overall score <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, while its sharpness is modulated
by a per-image consensus signal derived from the sub-dimension annotations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sub-dimension agreement signal.</span>
A fused image is uniformly easy to judge when all four sub-dimension
scores agree: thermal, texture, artifact, and sharpness ratings all
point to the same quality level—and is intrinsically more arguable when
they disagree, since different reasonable weightings of these criteria
would lead to different overall judgments. We capture this property with
the empirical standard deviation of the four sub-dimension scores:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}\;\triangleq\;\mathrm{std}\!\left(s_{i}^{(1)},\,s_{i}^{(2)},\,s_{i}^{(3)},\,s_{i}^{(4)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.180em;" width="1.180em"><mi>std</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}\;\triangleq\;\mathrm{std}\!\left(s_{i}^{(1)},\,s_{i}^{(2)},\,s_{i}^{(3)},\,s_{i}^{(4)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A small <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a consensual overall judgment; a large
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the four perceptual criteria disagree, so
that the overall score is less consensual across reasonable aggregation
rules. We empirically validate that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tracks genuine
annotation ambiguity, rather than merely reflecting multi-attribute
trade-off, in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-image label width.</span>
Following the Thurstone framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, we model
each fused image’s latent perceptual quality as a Gaussian on the
rating scale, parameterized by a per-image center <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and width
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> anchors the label at the overall
score; the width <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined to scale linearly with
the sub-dimension agreement signal <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on top of an irreducible
noise floor <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\;=\;\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\sigma_{0}+\lambda_{c}\,\delta_{i},\;\sigma_{\min},\,\sigma_{\max}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\;=\;\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\sigma_{0}+\lambda_{c}\,\delta_{i},\;\sigma_{\min},\,\sigma_{\max}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The floor <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> accounts for residual annotation noise even when
all four criteria agree (e.g., from discretizing continuous perceptual
judgments into the integer scale <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>); <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> controls
how sensitively the label width responds to sub-dimension disagreement;
and the soft clip <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\in[\sigma_{\min},\sigma_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\in[\sigma_{\min},\sigma_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>
prevents degenerate distributions at the extremes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian binning with first-moment preservation.</span>
Given the center <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we discretize a
Gaussian density over the five quality levels:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}(l)\;\propto\;\mathcal{N}\!\left(l;\,\mu_{i},\,\sigma_{i}^{2}\right),\qquad l\in\mathcal{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}(l)\;\propto\;\mathcal{N}\!\left(l;\,\mu_{i},\,\sigma_{i}^{2}\right),\qquad l\in\mathcal{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Following DeQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, we further post-adjust <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> so that its
first moment exactly recovers the overall score:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}p_{i}(l)=1,\qquad\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}l\cdot p_{i}(l)=\mu_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.000em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}p_{i}(l)=1,\qquad\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}l\cdot p_{i}(l)=\mu_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This first-moment preservation is essential: it ensures that the
distributional target <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the scalar target <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are mutually
consistent, so that minimizing the divergence between <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
also drives the predicted score <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}=\mathbb{E}_{q_{i}}[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}=\mathbb{E}_{q_{i}}[L]</annotation></semantics></math> toward
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, the per-image soft label is sharply concentrated
around <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> when the sub-dimensions agree, and broadens around <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
when they conflict, faithfully reflecting how consensual the overall
judgment is on each fused image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Tripartite Training Objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural choice would be to train FuScore by matching <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
alone. However, the primary metrics by which IQA models are evaluated
(<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, SRCC and KRCC) are non-differentiable
functions of the predicted ranking; per-image distributional alignment
only supervises ranking implicitly through each image’s expected score
and provides a weak signal in regimes where the GT <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-gap between
two images is small relative to label noise. We therefore add two
differentiable Thurstone-style <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> ranking proxies whose scopes mirror the two ranking metrics that the field reports.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.06969v1/method.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/152;" width="476" height="152" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Framework of our FuScore. a) FuScore Inference for per image. b) Sub-dim-aware soft label construction. c) Tripartite training process.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Within- and cross-source-pair scopes match the
evaluation metrics.</span>
The total variance of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a natural decomposition by source
pair <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(y)\;=\;\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{g}\!\left[\mathrm{Var}(y\,|\,g)\right]}_{\text{within-source-pair}}\;+\;\underbrace{\mathrm{Var}_{g}\!\left[\mathbb{E}(y\,|\,g)\right]}_{\text{cross-source-pair}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>within-source-pair</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>Var</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>cross-source-pair</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(y)\;=\;\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{g}\!\left[\mathrm{Var}(y\,|\,g)\right]}_{\text{within-source-pair}}\;+\;\underbrace{\mathrm{Var}_{g}\!\left[\mathbb{E}(y\,|\,g)\right]}_{\text{cross-source-pair}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The within-source-pair component captures how different fusion methods
perform on the same scene, the quantity that per-source-pair Kendall
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> measures at evaluation time, while the cross-source-pair
component captures how scene-level difficulty itself shifts the
achievable quality, which is what pooled SRCC reflects. We therefore train FuScore with three complementary terms whose summation scopes correspond to these two evaluation regimes plus the per-image shape signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-image distributional supervision <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
The first term aligns the predicted distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the soft
label <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via Kullback–Leibler divergence:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}\;=\;\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\mathrm{KL}\!\left(p_{i}\,\big\|\,q_{i}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}\;=\;\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\mathrm{KL}\!\left(p_{i}\,\big\|\,q_{i}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the batch size. By the first-moment preservation in
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), this term simultaneously supervises both the
shape of the level distribution and the location of the predicted
expectation <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Within-source-pair fidelity <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Within a source pair, “which fused image is better” is a semantically
well-defined comparison, since both fused images depict the same scene
and arise from the same content-preservation task. We therefore impose
a Thurstone-style pairwise fidelity loss on every pair <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> that
shares the same source pair <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(i)=g(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(i)=g(j)</annotation></semantics></math>. We use a single per-pair
margin</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{ij}\;\triangleq\;\sqrt{\sigma_{i}^{2}+\sigma_{j}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{ij}\;\triangleq\;\sqrt{\sigma_{i}^{2}+\sigma_{j}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">inherited from the soft-label widths of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), and
define the Ground Truth (GT) and predicted pairwise preference
probabilities as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_{i}-\mu_{j}}{\sigma_{ij}}\right),\qquad P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\hat{y}_{i}-\hat{y}_{j}}{\sigma_{ij}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_{i}-\mu_{j}}{\sigma_{ij}}\right),\qquad P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\hat{y}_{i}-\hat{y}_{j}}{\sigma_{ij}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard Gaussian CDF. We score the two
distributions with the DeQA-Score fidelity loss
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, which is bounded in <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> per pair and
self-saturates at confident pairs:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fid}}|}\!\!\ \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fid}}}\!\!\!\Big[1-\sqrt{P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}}-\sqrt{\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}\big)\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}\big)}\ \Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.327em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fid}}|}\!\!\ \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fid}}}\!\!\!\Big[1-\sqrt{P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}}-\sqrt{\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}\big)\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}\big)}\ \Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fid}}=\{(i,j):\,g(i)=g(j),\,i&lt;j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{fid}}=\{(i,j):\,g(i)=g(j),\,i&lt;j\}</annotation></semantics></math>
indexes the within-source-pair pairs in the batch.
The pairwise margin <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> widens when either image has a less
consensual overall judgment, so that high-<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> pairs contribute
looser comparisons and low-<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> pairs contribute sharper
ones—an uncertainty-aware reweighting that follows naturally from the
soft-label construction in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-source-pair Thurstone fidelity
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
The within-source-pair fidelity <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}</annotation></semantics></math> is restricted
to comparisons that share a scene; the cross-source-pair component of
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) – the variation in achievable quality across
<em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">different</em> scenes – is left unsupervised by
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}</annotation></semantics></math> alone. We
therefore extend the same Thurstone fidelity machinery of
Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) to all batch pairs <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> that span
<em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distinct</em> source pairs <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(i)\neq g(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(i)\neq g(j)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{xfid}}|}\!\!\ \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{xfid}}}\!\!\!\Big[1-\sqrt{P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}}-\sqrt{\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}\big)\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}\big)}\ \Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.327em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{xfid}}|}\!\!\ \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{xfid}}}\!\!\!\Big[1-\sqrt{P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}}-\sqrt{\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}\big)\big(1{-}P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}\big)}\ \Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{xfid}}=\{(i,j):\,g(i)\neq g(j),\,i&lt;j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{xfid}}=\{(i,j):\,g(i)\neq g(j),\,i&lt;j\}</annotation></semantics></math>
indexes the cross-source-pair pairs in the batch, and
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly the
quantities defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), reusing the
per-pair margin <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Because
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> widens whenever either image carries a less consensual
overall judgment, the score-side gradient <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}/\partial\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}/\partial\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> scales as
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sigma_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sigma_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, so high-<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> pairs automatically contribute
smaller score-update magnitudes per pair without a separate
down-weighting hyperparameter. The DeQA fidelity is
furthermore bounded in <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> per pair and reaches its minimum of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>
exactly at <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}\!=\!P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>pred</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}^{\mathrm{pred}}\!=\!P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared
to BCE, this avoids the unbounded log-domain penalty at
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}\!\to\!\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>gt</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}^{\mathrm{gt}}\!\to\!\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, so a small number of
near-confident pairs no longer dominate the loss; the training signal
stays distributed across both confident and ambiguous pairs.
Equivalently,
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same Thurstone fidelity applied with
the within-source-pair filter removed; it inherits all of
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s uncertainty-aware behaviour at the cost of
one additional summation scope, and it reuses the scalar readout
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> already computed in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Problem Setup ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), requiring no
additional forward pass. We discuss in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> why a
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-blind Plackett–Luce listwise alternative on the same scalar
readout regresses both ranking and calibration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full objective.</span>
Based on the above, the complete training loss is the weighted sum of the three terms:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\;=\;\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}\;+\;\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}\;+\;\lambda_{\mathrm{xfid}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\;=\;\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}\;+\;\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}\;+\;\lambda_{\mathrm{xfid}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments use EVAFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, an
expert-reviewed IVIF quality benchmark (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9{,}350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9,350</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9{,}350</annotation></semantics></math> fused images,
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="850" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>850</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">850</annotation></semantics></math> IR/visible source pairs, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> fusion methods, four
sub-dimension scores plus an overall score in
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>). Because the official split leaks over <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>
of source pairs across train/val/test boundaries, we
re-partition the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="850" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>850</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">850</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs into disjoint <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80/10/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80/10/10</annotation></semantics></math>
buckets, yielding <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="680/85/85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>680</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">680/85/85</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs and
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{,}480/935/935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7,480</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>935</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>935</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{,}480/935/935</annotation></semantics></math> images. FuScore instantiates
Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> fine-tuned with LoRA
(<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}64</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}128</annotation></semantics></math>) on the LM transformer blocks (vision
encoder frozen). Soft-label hyperparameters are
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}{=}0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}{=}0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}{=}0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}{=}0.45</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{\min},\sigma_{\max}]{=}[0.15,1.2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{\min},\sigma_{\max}]{=}[0.15,1.2]</annotation></semantics></math>; loss weights
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}{=}1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}{=}1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{xfid}}{=}0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{xfid}}{=}0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Training uses bf16 on a single A100-80G for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> epochs. We report SRCC, PLCC, KRCC, within-source-pair pairwise
accuracy (PairAcc), per-source-pair Kendall <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\|p_{\mathrm{GT}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>GT</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\|p_{\mathrm{GT}})</annotation></semantics></math> against the
soft-Gaussian target of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). For
uncertainty-aware variants we additionally report calibration
ECE under two post-hoc recalibrations and an oracle slope
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> summarising residual <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent
miscalibration (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\ast}{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\ast}{=}0</annotation></semantics></math> iff single-parameter rescaling is
optimal); full protocol in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Setup Details ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> Four classes, all on the same
source-pair-disjoint test split: <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">handcrafted</em> IVIF
metrics (EN, MI, VIF, SCD, Qabf, SF, AG, SD, SSIM);
<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">zero-shot natural-image MLLM-IQA</em>
(MUSIQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, CLIP-IQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>,
Q-Align <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, DeQA-Score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>);
<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same-backbone zero-shot</em> (Qwen3-VL-8B without
fine-tuning); and <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-domain learned</em>: (i)
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ViT-L+MLP</span>, the EVAFusion reward-model recipe re-trained
on our split, (ii) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA-MSE</span>, identical LoRA
configuration to FuScore but trained with L1 regression on
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}-y</annotation></semantics></math>, isolating whether gains come from backbone or
supervision recipe, and (iii) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA-CE</span>, the same
backbone trained with one-hot cross-entropy on five discrete
levels, isolating whether distribution-level supervision beats
discrete-token supervision. Implementation details for all
baselines in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Setup Details ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports score regression on the
EVAFusion test split. Our 3-seed ensemble outperforms every
external baseline on all five rank metrics, gaining <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.094" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.094</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.094</annotation></semantics></math>
SRCC, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.091" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.091</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.091</annotation></semantics></math> PLCC, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.076" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.076</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.076</annotation></semantics></math> KRCC, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.029" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.029</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.029</annotation></semantics></math> PairAcc, and
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.049" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.049</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.049</annotation></semantics></math> per-SP <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> over the strongest external baseline
ViT-L+MLP, all paired-bootstrap-significant at <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>
(<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{boot}}{=}2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>boot</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{boot}}{=}2000</annotation></semantics></math>). The same-backbone direct-regression
control (Qwen3-VL-8B + LoRA-MSE) attains SRCC <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.537" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.537</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.537</annotation></semantics></math>
against our <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.648" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.648</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.648</annotation></semantics></math>, isolating the contribution of our
soft-label + tripartite-loss recipe at <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.111" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.111</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.111</annotation></semantics></math> SRCC over
identical backbone capacity. None of the zero-shot natural-image
IQA scorers (Tab. <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, rows 4–8, spanning
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>M to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>B parameters) crosses SRCC <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.31</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.31</annotation></semantics></math> on IVIF,
indicating that natural-image IQA pretraining does not transfer
to IVIF without in-domain supervision. Beyond scalar regression,
our predicted level distribution attains KL <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.291" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.291</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.291</annotation></semantics></math> against the
soft-Gaussian GT, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="15{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> tighter than every
distribution-emitting baseline.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Score regression on EVAFusion test (<math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>935</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}935</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85</annotation></semantics></math>
source pairs). <span id="S4.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span>: best per column. “—”:
scalar-output methods. KL column:
<math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\|p_{\mathrm{GT}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>GT</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\|p_{\mathrm{GT}})</annotation></semantics></math> against the
soft-Gaussian target of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.11" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.11.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.11.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"></th>
<th id="S4.T1.11.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.11.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.11.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.11.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">SRCC<math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
