<th id="S4.T3.8.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ViT-L+MLP</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.8.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.471</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.478</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.8.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.354</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.8.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.714</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.8.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.8.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Ours (single-seed)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.8.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.547</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.551</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.9.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.413</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.9.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.731</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.8.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.8.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Ours<span id="S4.T3.8.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (3-seed ensemble)</span></span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.8.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">0.561</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.10.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">0.564</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.10.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">0.428</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.8.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.8.10.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">0.742</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build a 5-expert pilot benchmark on SemanticRT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, an IVIF dataset whose public release
ships fused images without quality annotations. Five domain
experts independently rate the overall quality of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1100</annotation></semantics></math> fused
images (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> fusion methods) on the same
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> scale used by EVAFusion, blinded to method identity;
the per-image GT is the mean of the five ratings (inter-rater
Kendall <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W{=}0.65" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W{=}0.65</annotation></semantics></math>). All scorers are evaluated in inference-only
mode using EVAFusion-trained or zero-shot weights; no Semantic
RT image enters training. Tab. <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Cross-dataset (SemanticRT 5-expert pilot). ‣ 4.3 Out-of-Distribution Generalization via Cross-Method and Cross-Dataset ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports
correlation with the expert-mean ranking. Our 3-seed ensemble
attains the highest SRCC (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.561" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.561</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.561</annotation></semantics></math>), KRCC (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.428" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.428</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.428</annotation></semantics></math>), and PairAcc
(<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.742" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.742</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.742</annotation></semantics></math>), outperforming the strongest external baseline
ViT-L+MLP (SRCC <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.471" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.471</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.471</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.090" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.090</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.090</annotation></semantics></math> SRCC (paired-bootstrap
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>). The single-seed configuration also ranks above every
external baseline, confirming the recipe generalises beyond
EVAFusion’s specific annotation distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Studies and Further Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We dissect FuScore along two axes: the loss-composition choices
that distinguish our recipe from the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed DeQA-Score
recipe (Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, left), and a per-image
dimensional-conflict stratification across <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> scoring methods
(Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, right). The first row of
the loss-composition panel, “<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed (DeQA recipe)”,
applies fixed <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> + bounded fidelity exactly as in
DeQA-Score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and can equivalently be read
as DeQA-Score’s loss recipe re-trained on our same backbone and
source-pair-disjoint split protocol; the comparison against our
final row therefore isolates the contribution of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict
supervision and cross-source-pair Thurstone fidelity over an
otherwise identical pipeline. Diagnostic configurations stacking
<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-blind Plackett–Luce listwise terms, alternative
listwise scopes, the full <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-sensitivity grid, and full
calibration tables are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
Unless marked <sup id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>, every cell is a 3-seed ensemble.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.06969v1/quali.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/259;" width="476" height="259" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Qualitative results on three representative source
pairs (SP1–SP3). <span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top:</span> fused images from <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> methods
grouped into top/mid/bottom-quality tertiles by GT <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>
(annotated). <span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom:</span> predicted scores from FuScore
(red), DeQA-Score, Q-Align, and ViT-L+MLP across all <math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>
methods (sorted by GT <math id="S4.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, desc.), plotted against human GT
(dashed). FuScore tracks the GT trajectory most closely,
including the steep drop on bottom-tertile methods.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-source-pair Thurstone fidelity is the rank
lever; <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict supervision installs a principled
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Adding <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math> on top of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict
supervision (Ours) lifts the three pooled correlation metrics by
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.012" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.012</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.012</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.014</annotation></semantics></math> SRCC over the <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict-only baseline
(Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> left: <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6337\to 0.6477" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6337</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.6477</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6337\to 0.6477</annotation></semantics></math>,
paired-bootstrap <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>), while leaving within-source-pair
PairAcc and per-SP <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.005</annotation></semantics></math>. Holding
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math> off and merely replacing fixed
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict supervision leaves all five rank
metrics within <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> of the DeQA-recipe baseline;
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict supervision is therefore not a rank lever in
isolation. Its role is to install a data-aligned <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>
that <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math> then exploits: the
predicted-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> correlation with the per-image dimensional
conflict <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> rises from <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}{-}0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.24</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}{-}0.24</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed
to <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.31</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict supervision, and to
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{+0.41}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">+</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{+0.41}</annotation></semantics></math> once <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math> is added
(Ours), with <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>’s mean shifting by less than <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>
across all three cells. Calibration tracks the
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-supervision strength: the oracle smooth-recal slope
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> – residual <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent miscalibration that
single-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> rescaling cannot exploit – drops from
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.59</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed to exactly <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on both
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-conflict-only and Ours, indicating that the predicted
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> already encodes the per-image scale information
that smooth recal would otherwise have to learn post-hoc. Full
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> statistics, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ECE}_{\tau^{\ast}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ECE</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ECE}_{\tau^{\ast}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ECE}_{\mathrm{smooth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ECE</mi><mi>smooth</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ECE}_{\mathrm{smooth}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and diagnostic <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-blind-PL
stacks in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The high-<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertile is a data-side floor across
<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> scoring methods.</span>

We complement the loss-composition ablation with a per-image
breakdown of the test set by dimensional conflict <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
(§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), splitting the <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>935</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">935</annotation></semantics></math> test images
into low-, mid-, and high-<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertiles.
Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (right) reports per-tertile
SRCC for four representative scoring methods spanning four
inductive-bias families: handcrafted IVIF statistics (Qabf),
zero-shot natural-image MLLM-IQA (DeQA-Score), an in-domain
ViT-L+MLP regressor, and our final model. The full <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>-method
extension covering MUSIQ, CLIP-IQA, VIF, MI, Q-Align, Qwen3-VL-8B
zero-shot, and our loss-composition cells is given in
Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and exhibits the same pattern.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the high-<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertile (the <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27\%</annotation></semantics></math> of test images on which
the four sub-dimension scores exhibit large internal
disagreement) SRCC collapses into a narrow band of
<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.140,0.254]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.140</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.254</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.140,0.254]</annotation></semantics></math> across <em id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> scoring methods we
evaluate</em> (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>,
Tab. <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.2 Eleven-Method 𝛿-Stratified Analysis ‣ Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) – a band an order of
magnitude narrower than each method’s own low- or mid-<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>
SRCC. Eleven methods spanning four orthogonal inductive-bias
families plateauing together is the signature of a data-side
ranking floor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The pattern admits a closed-form mechanical explanation.
EVAFusion’s released overall score satisfies
<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=\mathrm{mean}(s_{i}^{(1\ldots 4)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=\mathrm{mean}(s_{i}^{(1\ldots 4)})</annotation></semantics></math> exactly on every test
image, so high-<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> images have <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> pulled toward the centre
of the rating range and bunched there: empirically, the
high-<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertile’s GT <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> spans only <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2.25,3.75]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.75</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2.25,3.75]</annotation></semantics></math>
(std <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.222" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.222</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.222</annotation></semantics></math>) against the low-<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>’s <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2.25,4.25]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4.25</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2.25,4.25]</annotation></semantics></math>
(std <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.265" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.265</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.265</annotation></semantics></math>). The narrower GT-<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> range mechanically caps SRCC
for any scoring method, explaining the model-side floor. Where
within-tertile ranking signal does exist, our recipe contributes
most directly: on the mid-<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertile our model attains
<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.624" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.624</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.624</annotation></semantics></math> against the <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed baseline’s <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.597" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.597</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.597</annotation></semantics></math>, a
<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.027</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.027</annotation></semantics></math> gain that more than doubles the overall <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.015</annotation></semantics></math> delta
within the loss-composition grid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct human-side evidence: <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> correlates with
inter-rater disagreement on SemanticRT.</span>
The data-side floor argument above is necessary but not
sufficient for the claim that <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tracks human annotation
ambiguity rather than merely multi-attribute trade-off: the
<math id="S4.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>-method plateau on high-<math id="S4.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> samples shows these images
are hard to rank, but does not directly establish that human
raters themselves disagree more on these images. To close this
identification gap, we exploit the <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-expert raw ratings
collected for the SemanticRT pilot (§<a href="#S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" title="Cross-dataset (SemanticRT 5-expert pilot). ‣ 4.3 Out-of-Distribution Generalization via Cross-Method and Cross-Dataset ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>),
on which each expert independently rates both the four
IVIF-specific sub-dimensions and the overall quality. Raw
per-rater data are released for our pilot, unlike for EVAFusion.
For each of the <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1100</annotation></semantics></math> pilot images we compute the per-image
inter-rater disagreement on the overall score,
<math id="S4.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{std}_{5}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>std</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{std}_{5}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, across the five experts – a direct
operationalisation of human-side ambiguity – and correlate it
with the per-image dimensional conflict <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> computed from
the GT sub-dimension means on the same image. The two quantities
correlate at Pearson <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.42</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1100</annotation></semantics></math>),
indicating that high-<math id="S4.SS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> images are images on which trained
human raters themselves disagree more, not merely images on
which models happen to fail. Combined with the <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>-method
data-side floor on EVAFusion test and the GT-<math id="S4.SS4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> centroid
bunching mechanism, this provides the direct human-side evidence
promised by Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>: <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tracks
genuine annotation ambiguity, not merely multi-attribute
trade-off.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="S4.T4.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> loss-composition ablation; the
<math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed row equals the DeQA-Score recipe re-trained on
our backbone and split. <span id="S4.T4.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> SRCC stratified by
per-image conflict <math id="S4.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (tertile sizes <math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="468/218/249" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>468</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>218</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>249</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">468/218/249</annotation></semantics></math>);
full <math id="S4.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>-method version in App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
<span id="S4.T4.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span>: best per column.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S4.T4.17" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:200.1pt;">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.17.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.17.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.17.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">SRCC<math id="S4.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.17.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">PLCC<math id="S4.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.17.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">KRCC<math id="S4.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.17.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">PairAcc<math id="S4.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.17.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Per-SP <math id="S4.T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.17.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.17.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.17.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">LoRA-MSE (direct regression)</span></th>
