<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experimental Setup Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix expands the compressed setup of
Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> with full dataset, baseline, metric, and
implementation details.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Dataset and Re-Split Protocol</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted on EVAFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>,
the largest publicly released expert-reviewed IVIF quality
assessment benchmark, comprising <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9{,}350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9,350</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9{,}350</annotation></semantics></math> fused images organised
as <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="850" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>850</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">850</annotation></semantics></math> IR/visible source pairs each processed by <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> fusion
methods. Per-image annotations consist of four sub-dimension
scores – <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Thermal Retention</em>, <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Texture Preservation</em>,
<em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Artifacts</em>, <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sharpness</em> – on the integer scale
<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> together with a real-valued <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Overall
Score</em>; the public release does not expose raw per-rater records.
Because the official <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{,}350/1{,}000/1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7,350</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1,000</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{,}350/1{,}000/1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> split leaks
over <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math> of source pairs across train/val/test boundaries, we
re-partition the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="850" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>850</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">850</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs into disjoint <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80/10/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80/10/10</annotation></semantics></math>
buckets under seed <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="680/85/85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>680</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">680/85/85</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs
and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{,}480/935/935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7,480</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>935</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>935</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{,}480/935/935</annotation></semantics></math> images, respectively. Unless otherwise
noted, the released Overall Score serves as the scalar prediction
target <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in[1,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in[1,5]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Baseline Methods</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To isolate what the proposed recipe contributes on top of the
same backbone, we primarily compare against <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-VL-8B
zero-shot</span>, which uses the identical Qwen3-VL-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>
base model with no LoRA fine-tuning. We further include a learned
in-domain baseline obtained by reproducing the released EVAFusion
reward-model recipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and re-training it on
the same source-pair-disjoint train split used by our method,
ensuring a fair comparison under the same no-leakage protocol.
Concretely, this baseline is a <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ViT-L+MLP</span> regressor
trained under direct <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> regression. We additionally include
<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-VL-8B + LoRA-MSE</span>, a same-backbone direct-regression
control with identical LoRA configuration to FuScore but trained
with an L1 loss on the scalar <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}-y</annotation></semantics></math> rather than our
soft-label objective; this control isolates whether FuScore’s
gains stem from backbone capacity or from our supervision recipe.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also include four zero-shot perceptual quality scorers
pretrained on natural-image IQA, spanning a wide model-scale
spectrum, to probe natural-image-to-IVIF transfer:
<span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MUSIQ</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> (multi-scale ResNet+Transformer,
KonIQ-10k pretrained, <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!30</annotation></semantics></math>M parameters);
<span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP-IQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> (frozen CLIP RN50
backbone with antipodal “Good photo./Bad photo.” prompts,
<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!100</annotation></semantics></math>M parameters, no IQA-specific training);
<span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q-Align</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> (mPLUG-Owl2 + LLaMA-2 MLLM,
KonIQ-trained, <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!7</annotation></semantics></math>B parameters); and
<span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeQA-Score</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> (same backbone as
Q-Align, mix3-trained, <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!7</annotation></semantics></math>B parameters). Finally, we report
nine handcrafted IVIF metrics – EN, MI, VIF, SCD, Qabf, SF, AG,
SD, SSIM – used directly as standalone score predictors. All
baselines are evaluated on the same <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>935</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">935</annotation></semantics></math>-image
source-pair-disjoint test split as our method. CLIP-IQA and MUSIQ
are run via the <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pyiqa</span> package; Q-Align and DeQA-Score
are run from their official Hugging Face checkpoints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Metrics</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior IQA work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, we
report Spearman rank correlation (SRCC), Pearson linear
correlation (PLCC), and Kendall rank correlation (KRCC) between
predicted and ground-truth Overall Scores on the <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>935</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">935</annotation></semantics></math>-image
test set. To account for EVAFusion’s source-pair structure, we
additionally report within-source-pair pairwise accuracy and the
mean per-source-pair Kendall <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85</annotation></semantics></math> test source
pairs. For score-distribution prediction, we report the KL
divergence
<math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{GT}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>GT</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{GT}})</annotation></semantics></math> between
the predicted level distribution and the soft Gaussian-over-levels
target of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), averaged over the test set; the
eval-time direction is the reverse of the training loss in
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), providing a stricter test of distribution
match.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Calibration Protocol</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether the <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mu},\hat{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mu},\hat{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> summary of <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
– with <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}=\sum_{k}k\,p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}=\sum_{k}k\,p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\sum_{k}(k-\hat{\mu})^{2}p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\sum_{k}(k-\hat{\mu})^{2}p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> taken over <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> – forms a
calibrated predictive Gaussian on test, after a post-hoc
recalibration step fit on a held-out source-pair-disjoint
calibration split. We report two protocols.
<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ECE}_{\tau^{\ast}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ECE</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ECE}_{\tau^{\ast}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses single-parameter scaling
<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}=\tau^{\ast}\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}=\tau^{\ast}\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> fit by Brent search on a
single <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> calibration split, with split-induced variance on
the order of <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.005</annotation></semantics></math> at this sample size.
<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ECE}_{\mathrm{smooth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ECE</mi><mi>smooth</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ECE}_{\mathrm{smooth}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses two-parameter smooth
recalibration <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}=(a+b\hat{\sigma})\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}=(a+b\hat{\sigma})\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> fit by
Nelder–Mead, with the test ECE averaged over <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> independent
source-pair-disjoint cal/test splits; the oracle slope <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>
reported alongside is the average over the same <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> fits and
serves as a single-parameter mechanism summary
(<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\ast}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\ast}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> iff a single <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> scaling is the optimal
recalibrator). The two estimators are not on a common precision
footing and we treat their absolute values as characteristic of
distinct measurement regimes rather than directly subtractable
quantities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, for each protocol we partition the unit interval
into <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=10</annotation></semantics></math> nominal coverage levels <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{b}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{b}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, count the
empirical fraction
<math id="A1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{c}_{b}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i}\mathbf{1}\!\left[|y_{i}-\hat{\mu}_{i}|\leq\Phi^{-1}\!\big((1{+}c_{b})/2\big)\,\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}_{i}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}_{b}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i}\mathbf{1}\!\left[|y_{i}-\hat{\mu}_{i}|\leq\Phi^{-1}\!\big((1{+}c_{b})/2\big)\,\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}_{i}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>
of test images whose absolute residual lies within the
corresponding two-sided Gaussian interval, and report
<math id="A1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ECE}=\tfrac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}|\hat{c}_{b}-c_{b}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ECE</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ECE}=\tfrac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}|\hat{c}_{b}-c_{b}|</annotation></semantics></math>
on the recalibrated <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> as the base model
and fine-tune it with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> (<math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=64</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=128</annotation></semantics></math>,
dropout <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>), while keeping the visual encoder unchanged.
Each training example jointly feeds the IR / visible / fused
triplet into the model, and all images are resized so that the
longer side does not exceed <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> pixels. We optimize with
AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, using an initial learning rate of
<math id="A1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> warmup steps,
and cosine decay. Training uses bf16 mixed precision and
gradient checkpointing, with an effective batch size of <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>,
for <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> epochs on a single NVIDIA A100-80G GPU. The soft-label
hyperparameters of Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>)
are <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}=0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}=0.45</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}=0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}=0.15</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=1.2</annotation></semantics></math>. Loss weights are
<math id="A1.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{xfid}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{xfid}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unless otherwise noted, multi-seed results are obtained by
averaging the per-image <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mu},\hat{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mu},\hat{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> summary across
seeds <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{42,43,44\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>43</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>44</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{42,43,44\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Batches are emitted by a stratified sampler: each batch contains
<math id="A1.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs with <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> method-images per source pair
(micro-batch size <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, effective batch size <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> via
gradient-accumulation <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>), which guarantees
<math id="A1.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{2}\!\cdot\!m=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{2}\!\cdot\!m=12</annotation></semantics></math> within-source-pair pairs and
<math id="A1.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\!\cdot\!n^{2}\!=\!16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\!\cdot\!n^{2}\!=\!16</annotation></semantics></math> cross-source-pair pairs per micro-batch,
so neither <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>fid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{fid}}</annotation></semantics></math> nor
<math id="A1.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>xfid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{xfid}}</annotation></semantics></math> is ever empty under uniform random
sampling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.6 </span>Full Main Results Table</h3>

<div id="A1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tab. <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.6 Full Main Results Table ‣ Appendix A Experimental Setup Details ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> extends the main results table
of Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> (Tab. <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with the
six remaining handcrafted IVIF metrics (SSIM, SD, SF, EN, SCD,
AG). All cells use the same evaluation protocol as the main
text. The extended handcrafted methods all attain SRCC below
<math id="A1.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.21</annotation></semantics></math> – substantially weaker than Qabf, VIF, and MI shown in
the main text – and do not change any qualitative claim about
handcrafted-metric performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Full score regression results on EVAFusion test
(source-pair-disjoint split; <math id="A1.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>935</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=935</annotation></semantics></math> images, <math id="A1.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85</annotation></semantics></math> source pairs).
<span id="A1.T5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> marks the best in each column. “—”
marks methods that emit a scalar quality estimate rather than a
per-image quality distribution, so a comparable KL on the
soft-Gaussian-over-levels target is undefined for them. The KL
column reports <math id="A1.T5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{GT}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>GT</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\mathrm{pred}}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{GT}})</annotation></semantics></math> against the soft-Gaussian-over-levels target
with <math id="A1.T5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2 Sub-Dimension-Aware Soft Label Construction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>); for zero-shot baselines
this is computed on each method’s emitted level distribution,
while for both Ours rows it is computed by Gaussian-binning the
predicted <math id="A1.T5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mu},\hat{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mu},\hat{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> back to the same five-level
support.</figcaption>
