<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Tertile Boundary Choice</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The tertile boundaries used throughout the
<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratification analysis (<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 0.45</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.45&lt;\delta\leq 0.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.45</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.45&lt;\delta\leq 0.71</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0.71</annotation></semantics></math>) are computed once on
the test set and reused across all rows of
Tab. <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.2 Eleven-Method 𝛿-Stratified Analysis ‣ Appendix B Extended Ablation Studies ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. The low boundary is
<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.45</annotation></semantics></math> rather than the smallest possible non-zero std among
integer sub-dim scores (<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.433" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.433</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.433</annotation></semantics></math>) so that images with one
sub-dimension differing by one level land in the low tertile,
yielding the reported <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=468/218/249" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>468</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>218</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>249</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=468/218/249</annotation></semantics></math> split. We verified
that perturbing the low boundary by <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.02</annotation></semantics></math> shifts <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>
images between low and mid tertiles and does not change any
qualitative claim in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Studies and Further Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Counterfactual SRCC Ceiling</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The residual SRCC headroom not closed by our method is not
entirely a model-side limitation: the high-<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertile’s
contribution is bounded above by the internal consistency of
the human annotation itself. To quantify how much of the gap is
bridgeable, we compute a counterfactual ceiling that holds the
high-<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> tertile at its empirical SRCC floor (the
within-tertile rater-noise plateau, <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.21</annotation></semantics></math> across every
cell of our grid) and replaces the low- and mid-<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>
within-tertile predictions with their respective oracle ranks
before re-pooling SRCC over the full <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>935</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">935</annotation></semantics></math>-image test set. This
places the achievable SRCC on EVAFusion test at approximately
<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.78</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.78</annotation></semantics></math>, against which our model’s <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6477" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6477</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6477</annotation></semantics></math> closes a substantial
fraction of the bridgeable headroom over the strongest external
baseline (ViT-L+MLP, <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.554" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.554</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.554</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Alternative Listwise Scope: PL on the Level
Distribution</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural alternative to placing the cross-source-pair
Plackett–Luce listwise term on the scalar <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> readout is
to apply it directly to the <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-way predicted level distribution
<math id="A2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\Delta^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\Delta^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We tested three weights for this variant
(<math id="A2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{PL\text{-}dist}}\in\{0.05,0.1,0.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>PL</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dist</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{PL\text{-}dist}}\in\{0.05,0.1,0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math>)
within our training pipeline and observed a consistent
rank-vs-KL trap. The listwise gradient on the level distribution
pulls <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> toward shapes that maximise pairwise rank ordering,
which conflicts directly with the soft-Gaussian KL supervision
(§<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) and substantially inflates the
distribution-prediction loss. At
<math id="A2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{PL\text{-}dist}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>PL</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dist</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{PL\text{-}dist}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> – a weight at which
the scalar-readout listwise term already converges cleanly –
SRCC drops by <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.035" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.035</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.035</annotation></semantics></math> from the corresponding no-listwise
reference, mean KL inflates by <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 90\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and ECE rises by a
factor of three. Lowering the weight to <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> recovers
SRCC to within <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.005</annotation></semantics></math> of that reference but yields no
positive ranking gain. The cross-source-pair listwise term must
therefore operate on the scalar <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> readout to avoid
degrading the underlying distribution-prediction target – a
structural constraint that motivates the formulation in
Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Tripartite Training Objective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>One-at-a-time <math id="A2.T10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-sensitivity sweep around the
submitted recipe (single seed <math id="A2.T10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.T10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=935" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>935</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=935</annotation></semantics></math> test images).
L2 is the reference cell and reuses the published seed <math id="A2.T10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math>
checkpoint of Tab. <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bringing Multimodal Large Language Models to Infrared-Visible Image Fusion Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; L1, L3, L4, L5 are
independently retrained with the indicated weight changed.
Higher is better for all metrics.</figcaption>
